# The First Echo

In the age before sound knew its own name, there lived a lonely mountain spirit named Aria. She dwelt in silence so complete that even her footsteps made no noise against the ancient stone.

One day, a wandering musician climbed her peaks, seeking inspiration. When he reached the summit, he sang a single, perfect note into the void. Aria had never heard anything so beautiful. She reached out desperately to catch the sound, but it slipped through her fingers like water.

Heartbroken, she wept. Her tears fell into the mountain's deepest caves, and there, the sound had hidden itself, waiting. When her tears touched it, the note sang back to her—softer, sadder, but undeniably the same.

Overjoyed, Aria called out, and the mountain called back. She laughed, and laughter returned. She had discovered that she could keep sounds, not by grasping them, but by giving them space to return.

This is why mountains still catch our voices and send them home. Aria learned that some things must be released to be truly held, and in every echo, she reminds us: loneliness ends not in possession, but in conversation.
